\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\taxon{Example}
\title{Two turns of a tetrahedron}

\p{Represent the \vocabk{faces}{frap-000D} of a tetrahedron by subsets of #{\{1,2,3,4\}}.
We calculate \vocabk{facet moves}{frap-000L} from
\citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}. For the facet #{H=123},
take an element of the \vocabk{rotation subgroup}{frap-000K} with global extension #{\widehat\alpha=(123)}, fixing
#{4}. The vertex-edge records follow the cycle}
##{(1,12)\longmapsto(2,23)\longmapsto(3,13)\longmapsto(1,12).}

\tikzfig2{\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
\node (a) at (0,1.4) {$(1,12)$};
\node (b) at (1.7,-0.8) {$(2,23)$};
\node (c) at (-1.7,-0.8) {$(3,13)$};
\draw[->] (a) -- (b);
\draw[->] (b) -- (c);
\draw[->] (c) -- (a);
\node (d) at (0,-2) {$(4,14)$};
\draw[->] (d) edge[loop right] (d);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Every arrow is the local move #{\mu_{H,\alpha}}. The record
#{(4,14)} stays fixed because #{4\not\leq H}, although the global
shape symmetry sends it to #{(4,24)}. The diagram records this
\vocabk{permutation}{frap-000L} of \vocabk{incidence positions}{frap-000L},
rather than the tetrahedron's face graph.}

\p{For records whose second entry is a facet, the same move gives}
##{(1,124)\longmapsto(2,234),\qquad
    (12,124)\longmapsto(23,234),\qquad
    (4,124)\longmapsto(4,124).}
\p{The last record is fixed even though the global symmetry changes
its second entry. Now let #{K=124} and take an element of the \vocabk{rotation subgroup}{frap-000K} with global
extension #{\widehat\beta=(124)}. Applying the #{H}-move and then the
#{K}-move sends}
##{(1,124)\xrightarrow{\mu_{H,\alpha}}(2,234)
     \xrightarrow{\mu_{K,\beta}}(4,134).}
\p{Reversing their order gives
#{(1,124)\mapsto(2,124)\mapsto(3,234)}, so the two moves do not commute.
Each has order three: its cube is the identity on both branches, and its
displayed action is nontrivial.}
